## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model data, persist with Room, export data offline, and share via intents. Emphasizes file access patterns and compatibility.

## Key Concepts
- Room: Entity, DAO, and a simple Repository
- JSON serialization of data
- FileProvider and content URIs
- Sharing via ACTION_SEND
- Runtime permissions considerations on older Android versions

## Code Example
```kotlin
@Entity(tableName = "notes")
data class Note(
  @PrimaryKey val id: Int,
  val title: String,
  val content: String,
  val createdAt: Long
)

@Dao interface NoteDao { @Query("SELECT * FROM notes ORDER BY createdAt DESC") fun getAllNotes(): List<Note> }

class NoteRepository(private val context: Context, private val dao: NoteDao) {
  fun exportNotes(): Uri {
    val notes = dao.getAllNotes()
    val json = Gson().toJson(notes)
    val dir = context.getExternalFilesDir(null)!!
    val file = File(dir, "notes.json")
    file.writeText(json)
    return FileProvider.getUriForFile(context, "com.example.fileprovider", file)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle large note datasets to avoid O(n) memory usage during export?
- How would you test FileProvider permissions and share intent across Android versions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical Compose performance debugging, not just theory.

## Key Concepts

- Compose recomposition profiling
- Stable keys in LazyColumn
- Extracting item composables
- Immutable data models with stable ids
- Derived state and remember patterns
- SnapshotFlow and diffing for list updates

## Code Example

```kotlin
data class Article(val id: String, val title: String, val body: String)

@Composable
fun ArticleRow(article: Article) {
  Text(text = article.title)
}

@Composable
fun NewsFeed(articles: List<Article>) {
  LazyColumn { 
    items(articles, key = { it.id }) { article ->
      ArticleRow(article)
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure the impact of introducing a DiffUtil-like mechanism for the list?
- What edge cases could cause misleading recomposition counts and how would you guard against them?

